# CODEOWNERS — who gets routed a PR, per path.
#
# ROUTING, NOT A GATE. Approval happens outside GitHub — see CONTRIBUTING.md,
# Approval process: AI-AskOps triage names a business owner and an IT owner,
# the IT owner collects the approvals and attests, and a repo admin merges.
# What these lines do is put the right people's names on the review request so
# the PR reaches them at all. They do not block a merge.
#
# Two things still depend on them, which is why the paths are kept accurate:
#   - scripts/governance_summary.py reads this file to work out who the
#     BUSINESS owner is for a changed path, which is the one approval the
#     process does ask for in GitHub, and what the intake card announces.
#   - A reviewer who is auto-requested is far likelier to look than one who
#     has to be found.
#
# PREREQUISITE: the @gatesfoundation/* teams below must exist, which requires
# this repo to live in the gatesfoundation GitHub org (personal repos can't
# use org teams), and each team needs write access — GitHub ignores a line
# whose owner lacks it, silently.
#
# THE TWO ROLES, DELIBERATELY ON DIFFERENT PATHS:
#   - Domain / business owner: the group's leads, SOLE owner of their group's
#     skill paths. For foundation-wide and shared-workflows skills no separate
#     group exists, so the Skills Working Group is the domain owner there too.
#   - Skills Working Group: owner of the publication registry
#     (/.claude-plugin/marketplace.json) and of catalog-level process files.
#
# Keep the Working Group's slug in step with GOVERNANCE_TEAM in
# scripts/governance_summary.py. That script subtracts the Working Group from a
# path's owners to find the business owner, so a mismatch makes every owner
# look like a business owner — and the intake card then names whoever
# approved first.
#
# WHY THE WORKING GROUP IS NOT LISTED ON GROUP SKILL PATHS:
#   Multiple owners on one CODEOWNERS line are OR, not AND, so listing both
#   teams on /plugins/groups/idm/ would have routed to either rather than
#   both. Keeping the roles on separate paths is what lets the business owner
#   be derived from the path at all.

# Default owner for anything no more specific line claims
#   CODEOWNERS is last-match-wins, so EVERY rule below overrides this one
*                        @gatesfoundation/skills-working-group
#
# RESIDUAL LIMIT (do not paper over this):
#   Someone in BOTH teams satisfies both owners with a single approval, and the
#   second of the two required approvals may come from anyone with write access.

# --- Catalog-level / process files → Skills Working Group ---
/.github/                @gatesfoundation/skills-working-group
/.claude-plugin/         @gatesfoundation/skills-working-group
/scripts/                @gatesfoundation/skills-working-group
/scanner/                @gatesfoundation/skills-working-group
/CONTRIBUTING.md         @gatesfoundation/skills-working-group
/README.md               @gatesfoundation/skills-working-group
/docs/                   @gatesfoundation/skills-working-group

# --- Foundation-wide skills → Skills Working Group (domain + governance board) ---
/plugins/foundation-wide/        @gatesfoundation/skills-working-group

# --- Group skills → the group's leads ONLY (domain owner) ---
# The board is deliberately absent here; see "WHY THE BOARD IS NOT LISTED ON
# GROUP SKILL PATHS" at the top. It still gates publication via /.claude-plugin/.
# Only teams that exist AND have write access to this repo are valid code owners;
# an unknown owner disables the whole line. A new group's <group>-leads line stays
# COMMENTED until that team is created and granted repo write access — then just
# uncomment the matching line (added by the PR that creates the group's directory).
#
# The catch-all below comes FIRST on purpose. CODEOWNERS is last-match-wins, so
# every per-team line beneath it overrides it. Its job is the gap that opens the
# moment a team ships before its own line exists: without it, plugins/groups/<new>/
# matches nothing and has NO required reviewer at all — the skills merge ungoverned
# and nothing reports it. A missing team line should cost a broader reviewer, not
# no reviewer.
/plugins/groups/                     @gatesfoundation/skills-working-group
/plugins/groups/idm/                 @gatesfoundation/idm-leads

# --- Shared workflows → Skills Working Group (no single team owns these) ---
# An unrouted path has no required reviewer at all, which is the opposite of
# what a centrally owned plugin should have. Where a shared plugin has a named
# lead team, add a more specific line BELOW this one — CODEOWNERS is
# last-match-wins, so a specific line above a general one is ignored.
/plugins/shared-workflows/       @gatesfoundation/skills-working-group
